== fuller page ==
C4 keeps its words in rooms, and every room is one letter. There are
seventeen [seventeen]. They live in a plain text file called m/t, one line
for each letter [mt]. Each line gives its letter four names, under four
columns that have names of their own: scalar, group, command and tag [faces].
A's line reads ASSET, ARRAY, ADD, ARCHIVE. S's line reads SCALAR, SPOKE,
SELECT, SEMAPHORE, and that is where Spoke got a name [srow].

That makes sixty-eight words. Sixty-four of them are common English; only
four (asset, obfuscate, spoke and taxonomy) are claimed nowhere else [common].
The busy words are on purpose: when two rooms both need the word "map", the
letter keeps them apart [namespace].

Nine more letters hold values and force stops [nine]. Pin has two notes about
them that differ by one word: one says Q is QUERY, the table says
QUERY-STABLE. Both are kept, because the difference is worth knowing.

Saying one idea four ways is not a muddle. C4 asks for at least two ways to
say everything, and asks that they balance [two]. The facing pages of this
book try to do the same.

